REGIONAL REGULATION OF SEMARANG CITY

NUMBER 5 OF 2016

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

MAYOR OF SEMARANG,

Considering:

a. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights and protection of human rights of women and children against acts of violence is one of the values contained in Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution;
b. that violence against women and children in Semarang City continues to increase and spread, causing community members to feel unsafe in carrying out their lives, thus requiring integrated protection efforts;
c. that to provide legal direction and certainty to all those involved in efforts to provide legal protection, it is necessary to regulate the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation of Semarang City concerning the Protection of Women and Children From Acts of Violence.

In View of:

1. Article 18 Paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions within the Province of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta;
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 Concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 39 of 2004 concerning the Protection of Indonesian Migrant Workers Abroad (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 133, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4445);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 5602)
7. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
8. Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 11 of 2012 Concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679)
10. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
11. Presidential Regulation Number 8 of 2014 concerning the Protection of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 44);
12. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 3 of 2009 Concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children (Regional Gazette of 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette number 20);
13. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 7 of 2013 Concerning the Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette Number 2013 Number 7, Supplement to the Regional Gazette Number 53).

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SEMARANG CITY

And

MAYOR OF SEMARANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and children from acts of violence is organized based on the principles of:
a. Humanity;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Non-discrimination;
d. Order and legal certainty;
e. Transparency;
f. Protection.

Article 3

The objectives of protection of women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. Prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. Eliminate all forms of acts of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. Protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. Provide services to women and children from acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. Empower women and children victims of violence to be more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.

CHAPTER III

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 4

Forms of violence include:
a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence;
d. Neglect;
e. Maltreatment;
f. Exploitation; and/or
g. Other violence.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. Right to be respected for their dignity as human beings;
b. Right to recovery;
c. Right to determine their own decisions;
d. Right to obtain information;
e. Right to confidentiality;
f. Right to social rehabilitation;
g. Right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. Right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
i. Right to assistance; and
j. Right to a sense of security.

Article 6

Child victims of acts of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 5, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. Right to survival, growth and development;
b. Right to basic population services;
c. Right to equal protection;
d. Right to be free from various stigma; and
e. Right to obtain freedom.

Article 8

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 letter a, include:
a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation activities in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutional protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation;
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Mayor shall establish programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 10

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obliged to make efforts in accordance with their capacity to:
a. Prevent and stop the continuation of acts of violence;
b. Provide protection to victims;
c. Provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. Assist in the process of submitting an application for protection determination.

Article 15

1. The Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every sub-district.

Article 16

1. The prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 15, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration:f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual; and
j. peace and order.
2. The prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Long-Term Development Plan and the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.

Article 17

Legal protection includes:
a. Providing protection in a safe house (shelter);
b. Providing legal information to victims;
c. Providing assistance for victims as witnesses from the investigation process to the verdict;
d. Providing special legal protection for child victims of acts of violence can be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

Article 18

Recovery includes:
a. Providing physical recovery at health service institutions;
b. Providing medicolegal services;
c. Assisting the repatriation of victims;
d. Providing temporary protection in a safe house (shelter);
e. Providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. Providing spiritual guidance services;
g. Preparing family, school, work and community environments, as well as economic empowerment.

Article 20

Community Participation is carried out by means of:
a. Fostering community awareness of cases of violence against women and children;
b. Encouraging the community to actively participate in providing information and reporting acts of violence against women and children;
c. Fostering local wisdom in handling cases of violence;
d. Organizing strengthening of community groups in handling violence against women and children;
e. Disseminating information about laws and regulations related to violence against women and children.

Article 21

1. The Regional Government develops cooperation patterns in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out between the Regional Government and:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. Universities;
e. Social and religious institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
4. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations

Article 22

1. The Regional Government may organize partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from violence;
2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
a. notification of employment opportunity information for women victims of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have been withdrawn from their education; and
d. fostering and increasing the economic independence of women victims of violence.
3. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IX

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 23

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from violence.
2. Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. coordination;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.

CHAPTER X

FINANCING

Article 26

Financing for the implementation of protection of women and children from violence may be sourced from:
a. State Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
b. Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
c. Non-binding community donations;
d. Non-binding foreign aid; and/or
e. Other lawful and non-binding sources in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
